<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<pretext>
  <article>
    <title>Kernel Mustard</title>

    <section>
      <title>Review</title>
      <ul>
        <li><p>What was the statement of the Steinitz lemma?</p></li>
        <li><p>What were we trying to use it to prove?</p></li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
      <title>Finishing Bases</title>
      <theorem xml:id="thm-BaseSize">
        <statement>
          <p>Every basis of a finite dimensional vector space has the same size.</p>
        </statement>
        <proof>
          <title>Proof of <xref ref="thm-BaseSize"/></title>
          <p>Let <m>B_{1}</m> and <m>B_{2}</m> be two bases, and suppose for the sake of contradiction that <m>|B_{1}| \lt |B_{2}|</m>. Let <m>S</m> be a subset of <m>B_{2} \setminus B_{1}</m> of maximal size such that there exists <m>T \subseteq B_{1} \setminus B_{2}</m> such that <m>|S| = |T|</m> and <m>(B_{1} \setminus T) \cup S</m> is a basis. Such a set exists, since one can take <m>S = \emptyset</m>. Note that</p>
          <p><md>|(B_{1} \setminus T) \cup S| = |B_{1}| \lt |B_{2}|.</md></p>
          <p>Thus, there exists <m>\vec{v} \in B_{2} \setminus ((B_{1} \setminus T) \cup S)</m>. Then by the Steinitz lemma, there exists <m>\vec{u} \in (B_{1} \setminus T) \cup S</m> such that <m>(B_{1} \setminus (T \cup \vec{u})) \cup S \cup \{\vec{v}\}</m> is a basis, a contradiction to the maximality of <m>S</m>.</p>
        </proof>
      </theorem>
    </section>

    <section>
      <title>Linear Transformations</title>

      <p>We have talked about vector spaces and now know that vector spaces have bases. Part of the purpose of bases is they give you a way to identify vector spaces and move between them.</p>

      <definition>
        <p>A <term>linear transformation</term> <m>T: V \to W</m>, where <m>V</m> and <m>W</m> are vector spaces, is a function such that for any <m>\vec{u}, \vec{v} \in V</m> and <m>\lambda \in \mathbb{R}</m>, <m>T(\vec{u} + \vec{v}) = T(\vec{u}) + T(\vec{v})</m> and <m>T(\lambda \vec{u}) = \lambda T(\vec{u})</m>.</p>
      </definition>

      <p>That is, a linear transformation is a function that preserves the only two things we can are guaranteed to be able to do in a vector space, scaling and adding.</p>

      <example>
        <p>Any map of the form <m>T: \mathbb{R}^{n} \to \mathbb{R}^{m}</m> given by <m>T(\vec{x}) = A \vec{x}</m> for <m>A</m> and <m>m \times n</m> matrix is a linear transformation.</p>
      </example>

      <example>
        <p>Them map <m>\text{ev}_{1}</m> from real valued functions given by <m>\text{ev}_{1}(f) = f(1)</m> is a linear transformation.</p>
      </example>

      <example>
        <p>The map <m>\frac{d}{dx}</m> from differentiable functions to functions is a linear transformation, since</p>
        <p><md>\frac{d}{dx}(f(x) + g(x)) = \frac{df}{dx}(x) + \frac{dg}{dx}(x)  \text{ and } \frac{d}{dx}\lambda f(x) = \lambda \frac{d}{dx}f(x).</md></p>
      </example>

      <p>Once you know a function is a linear transformation, you learn some important properties of that map for free.</p>

      <theorem>
        <statement>
          <p>Let <m>T: V \to W</m> be a linear transformation between two vector spaces <m>V</m> and <m>W</m>. Then <m>T(\vec{0}_{V}) =\vec{0}_{W}</m>.</p>
        </statement>
        <proof>
          <p>Note that <m>T(\vec{0}_{V}) = T(0(\vec{0}_{V})) = 0 T(\vec{0}_{V}) = \vec{0}_{W}.</m></p>
        </proof>
      </theorem>

      <p>For example, the derivative of the <m>0</m> function is <m>0</m>, and multiplying a matrix be the zero vector always yields <m>\vec{0}</m>. This is a uniform proof of both phenomena.</p>

      <p>In fact, a stronger statement is true. For any linear transformation <m>T: V \to W</m>, we define <m>\ker(T) = \{\vec{x} \in V: T(\vec{x}) =\vec{0}_{W}\}</m>, the pre-image of <m>\vec{0}_{W}</m>. We just showed that <m>\vec{0}_{V} \in \ker(T)</m>. Recall that is one of the three properties for being a linear subspaces.</p>

      <theorem>
        <statement>
          <p>For any linear transformation <m>T:V \to W</m>, <m>\ker(T)</m> is a linear subspace of <m>V</m>.</p>
        </statement>
        <proof>
          <p>We just showed <m>\vec{0}_{V} \in \ker(T)</m>. Thus, it suffices to show closure under addition and scalar multiplication. Let <m>\vec{x}, \vec{y} \in \ker(T)</m>. Then</p>
          <p><md>T(\vec{x} + \vec{y}) = T(\vec{x}) + T(\vec{y}) = \vec{0}_{W} + \vec{0}_{W} = \vec{0}_{W}.</md></p>
          <p>Let <m>\lambda \in \mathbb{R}</m>. Then</p>
          <p><md>T(\lambda \vec{x}) = \lambda T(\vec{x}) = \lambda\vec{0}_{W} = \vec{0}_{W}.</md></p>
          <p>Thus, <m>\vec{x}+\vec{y}, \lambda \vec{x} \in \ker(T)</m>. Therefore, by definition, <m>\ker(T)</m> is a linear subspace.</p>
        </proof>
      </theorem>

      <p>This fact gives a very useful way to build various linear subspaces. For example, this shows that functions with derivative <m>0</m> are a linear subspace. These are exactly the constant functions, which indeed are preserved under scaling and addition. It also shows that for any <m>m \times n</m> matrix, the set of <m>\vec{x} \in \mathbb{R}^{n}</m> such that</p>
      <p><md>A\vec{x} = \vec{0}</md></p>
      <p>is always a linear subspace. The power of this general statement is we get a proof of both facts simultaneously all as a consequence of this fundamental idea.</p>

      <p>Kernels also tell us about a linear transformation. Recall from 2020 that a function <m>f</m>is <term>one-to-one</term> if each input has a distinct output. That is <m>f(x) = f(y)</m> implies <m>x = y</m>.</p>

      <theorem>
        <statement>
          <p>Let <m>T:V \to W</m> be a linear transformation between vector spaces <m>V</m> and <m>W</m>. Then <m>T</m> is one-to-one if and only if <m>\ker(T) = \{\vec{0}_{V}\}</m>.</p>
        </statement>
        <proof>
          <p>(<m>\Longrightarrow</m>) Suppose that <m>T</m> is one-to-one. Note that <m>T(\vec{0}_{V}) = \vec{0}_{W}</m>. Since <m>T</m> is one-to-one, <m>\vec{0}_{V}</m> is the only vector for which that is the case. Therefore, <m>\ker(T) = \vec{0}_{V}</m>.</p>
          <p>(<m>\Longleftarrow</m>) Suppose that <m>\ker(T) = \{\vec{0}_{V}\}</m>. Let <m>\vec{u}, \vec{v} \in V</m>, and suppose that <m>T(\vec{u}) =T(\vec{v})</m>. To prove <m>T</m> is one-to-one, we want to then show that <m>\vec{u} = \vec{v}</m>. To argue this, observe that</p>
          <p><md>T(\vec{u}-\vec{v}) = T(\vec{u}) - T(\vec{v}) = \vec{0}_{W}.</md></p>
          <p>Thus, <m>\vec{u} - \vec{v} \in \ker(T)</m>. Therefore, since <m>\ker(T) = \{\vec{0}_{V}\}</m>, <m>\vec{u} - \vec{v} = \vec{0}_{V}</m>, so <m>\vec{u} = \vec{v}</m>. Therefore, <m>T</m> is one-to-one.</p>
        </proof>
      </theorem>

      <p>Linear transformations give rise to another set called their <term>image</term> denoted</p>
      <p><md>\text{Im}(T) = \{T(\vec{x}): \vec{x} \in V\}.</md></p>

      <theorem>
        <statement>
          <p>Let <m>T: V \to W</m> be a linear transformation from a vector space <m>V</m> to a vector space <m>W</m>. Then <m>\text{Im}(T)</m> is a linear subspace of <m>W</m>.</p>
        </statement>
        <proof>
          <p>Note that <m>\vec{0}_{W} = T(\vec{0}_{V}) \in \text{Im}(T)</m>. Thus, the image contains the zero vector.</p>
          <p>Let <m>\vec{x}, \vec{y} \in \text{Im}(T)</m>. Then there exist <m>\vec{u}, \vec{v} \in V</m> such that <m>T(\vec{u}) = \vec{x}</m> and <m>T(\vec{v}) = \vec{y}</m>. It follows that</p>
          <p><md>\vec{x} + \vec{y} = T(\vec{u}) + T(\vec{v}) = T(\vec{u} + \vec{v}) \in \text{Im}(T).</md></p>
          <p>Therefore, <m>\text{Im}(T)</m> is closed under addition.</p>
          <p>Let <m>\lambda \in \mathbb{R}</m>. Then</p>
          <p><md>\lambda \vec{x} = \lambda T(\vec{x}) = T(\lambda \vec{x}) \in \text{Im}(T).</md></p>
          <p>Therefore, <m>\text{Im}(T)</m> is closed under scalar multiplication and is thus a linear subspace.</p>
        </proof>
      </theorem>
    </section>

    <section>
      <title>Exercises</title>

      <p>Recall that a function <m>T: V \to W</m> is called <term>onto</term> if its range is <m>W</m>.</p>

      <exercise>
        <p>Explain why for a linear transformation <m>T: V \to W</m> between vector spaces <m>V</m> and <m>W</m>, <m>\text{Im}(T) = W</m> if and only if <m>T</m> is onto.</p>
      </exercise>

      <p>Part of the purpose of the introduction of kernel and images is to give you new ways to build linear subspaces.</p>

      <exercise>
        <p>Let <m>S</m> denote the set of vectors <m>(x,y) \in \mathbb{R}^{2}</m> such that <m>x = y</m>. Is <m>S</m> a linear subspace? If yes, find a linear transformation <m>T: \mathbb{R}^{2} \to \mathbb{R}^{2}</m> for which <m>S = \ker(T)</m>. Otherwise, prove why not.</p>
      </exercise>

      <exercise>
        <p>Let <m>S</m> denote the set of vectors <m>(x,y) \in \mathbb{R}^{2}</m> such that <m>x = y +1</m>. Is <m>S</m> a linear subspace? If yes, find a linear transformation <m>T: \mathbb{R}^{2} \to \mathbb{R}^{2}</m> for which <m>S = \ker(T)</m>. Otherwise, prove why not.</p>
      </exercise>

      <exercise>
        <p>Prove that the set of functions that are their own derivative is a linear subspace.</p>
      </exercise>

      <exercise>
        <p>Let <m>T: U \to V</m> and <m>T': V\to W</m> be linear transformations between vector spaces. Show that <m>\text{Im}(T) \subseteq \ker(T')</m> if and only if <m>T' \circ T:U \to W</m> is the <m>0</m>-function.</p>
      </exercise>
    </section>
  </article>
</pretext>
